4 — B6 LAZY ret_table
---

What changed
  Two walkers only:

  | File | Entry | Build |
  |------|-------|-------|
  | `tc_call_let.oo` `typecheck_let_ann_call_init` | :20 `let mut ret_table: String = ""` | :77-78 first non-cap miss |
  | `tc_call_ret.oo` `typecheck_return_and_assign_calls` | :14 same `""` | :131-132 `return f();` miss; :185-186 mut assign miss |

  Annotated `let x: T = cap(...)` hits `cap_builtin_ret` and skips the
  table. Unannotated `let x = cap(...)` also hits the helper and
  **drops** `gty` (dead store). `tc_call_ret.oo` is 256 lines (at the
  soft lock). HYG four: no unit over 256.

Why it matters
  Entry-time concat is gone in the two named files. Cap-only lets in
  those walkers do not pay `build_fn_ret_table`. That is the owner
  bar. PV1: PASS on that bar.

What is not done
  Lazy is defer, not delete. First non-cap miss still calls
  `build_fn_ret_table` and still concatenates the full `fname:Ret`
  String (`tc_tables_ret.oo`: `table = table + …`). O(F²) copies.
  The two walkers do not share a table.
  Siblings still build at entry (PV1 + MEM named list):
  `tc_ann_prep.oo:16`, `tc_call_arg_prep.oo:15`, `tc_call_eq.oo:14`,
  `tc_call_order.oo:14`, `tc_call_binop.oo:14`, `tc_call_logic.oo:14`,
  `tc_struct_method.oo:15`, `tc_ops_unary.oo:15` and `:92`.
  In-loop rebuilds: `tc_tables.oo:208` (inside `build_pure_lit_env`,
  which ret still runs at entry), `tc_control_cond.oo:200`.
  Unannotated non-cap `let x = foo()` never types and never builds.
  `cap_builtin_ret` still returns bare "Result" / "List" / "Bool" /
  "Int". Split vs `tc_names.oo` (`oo_file_size` Bool vs Int) stands.
  `match_rparen` still runs before the short-circuit on `return f();`.
  Cheap is unproven. No new `oodac check oodac/main.oo` this wave.

  Last INT clock (not re-timed here):
  sprint_int_close_open.oot 26.75s / 12.26 GiB RSS, rc=0.
  That is still over F26 WARN 8 GiB and under FAIL 32 GiB.
  **12 GiB is not closed.**

